## 📌 Overview

This research investigates the **performance vs. cost tradeoff** of Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) across two environments — **Minikube** (local) and **Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)** (cloud). Using Google's Online Boutique microservices demo app and Locust for load testing, we conducted **36 experiments** varying CPU thresholds, user loads, and autoscaling scopes to identify the optimal configuration for different use cases.

---

## 🔑 Key Findings

- 🏆 **GKE outperformed Minikube** across all high-load scenarios, offering superior scalability and stability
- ✅ **70% CPU threshold** delivered the best balance between performance and resource efficiency — though the ideal threshold ultimately depends on the specific application requirements
- ⚠️ **Minikube struggled at 500 concurrent users**, showing significant performance degradation and errors, making it unsuitable for production-level workloads
- 💡 **Minikube remains a viable option** for development and low-to-medium load scenarios (100–300 users) where cost is a priority
- 📊 **Multi-service HPA** provided more granular and efficient autoscaling compared to single-service configurations

---

## 🧪 Experiment Design

| Parameter | Values |
|-----------|--------|
| Environments | Minikube, GKE |
| CPU Thresholds | 50%, 70%, 85% |
| Concurrent Users | 100, 300, 500 |
| Autoscaling Scope | Single-service, Multi-service |
| Total Experiments | 36 (18 Minikube + 18 GKE) |
| Load Testing Tool | Locust 2.43 |

